---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PanelHead from '../../components/PanelHead.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Aping — Andrew Shiau"
  description="An agent skill that sets documents in the Swiss style, and the line I drew about whose style to copy."
>
  <!-- THE SPLIT (chooser round 2: concept 02, scope 03). Same `.pin` /
       `.splitrule` / `.feed` children at the same 4:8 columns as the index, so the
       site's one vertical division doesn't move when you click into a case study. -->
  <div class="grid">
    <PanelHead href="/work/aping/" title="Aping">
      <!-- TYPE B: no ledger in the panel. This page's facts block is a COLOPHON — what the
           thing is made of, read AFTER the work (STYLE.md) — so it stays at the foot of the
           reading column and the panel is marker + title + contact. The empty space under a
           three-item panel is the same condition the index's own panel is in; a ledger
           invented to fill it would be the one fabricated thing on the page. -->
    </PanelHead>

    <div class="feed">
      <!-- c1–12 of the FEED's twelve sub-tracks, not c1–8: the dek's own 32em cap is
           narrower than 8 of the feed's 700px, so c1–8 would set a column the text
           never reaches. No top margin — the dek is the top of the reading column. -->
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="dek c1-12">
          An agent skill that writes HTML in the Swiss style, on Müller-Brockmann’s grid. You
          invoke it and the agent sets your content by that system’s rules instead of the flat
          default it reaches for on its own.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE B, beat 2: a NAMED head over the page's argument. This used to be an unheaded
           paragraph, which made the best writing on the page look like a stray thought. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>The line I drew</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              Aping means imitating, and it’s the honest word for this. I taught an agent to
              set documents in a style I admire, and the pages it produces hold up.
            </p>
            <p>
              I started a second skill aimed at a studio that’s still working today, and
              stopped. Müller-Brockmann died in 1996; copying a movement is what design
              history is made of. Pointing a machine at a living studio’s look and shipping
              the output is something else, and I didn’t like the sound of it once I’d said it
              out loud. So the rule is: the dead, or a movement. Nobody I could have asked.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid">
        <div class="block c1-6">
          <span class="st">This site is set in the /swiss style.</span>
          <span class="sd">Press g to see the grid under it.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- No paragraph follows this head — the .step below is the content — so there's
           nothing to pair on the same row. Flush left, same column-1 start as a .shead's h2
           so every head on the site still lines up. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="c1-12"><h2>The style</h2></div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
        <div class="step accent" style="grid-column:span 8">
          <span class="sl">after Josef Müller-Brockmann (1914–1996)</span>
          <span class="st">/swiss</span>
          <span class="sd">A visible modular grid carrying large, precisely-set type. Structure comes from column alignment and thin rules; there are no boxes. One accent colour. This site is set in it.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- The missing media (chooser round 2, Q3/03), and it is an EMBED rather than a
           recording. The claim above is "the agent sets a document by that system's rules" —
           the evidence for it is a document the agent set, running. A screen recording of a
           static page would be a video of something not moving, and a screenshot would be a
           picture of a page you can open. The `figure.embed` device already ships on
           `recipes` and `proofs`; this page had no visual evidence of any kind, which made
           it the emptiest page in the set.
           Canelé, not the 10x essay: this page's subject is the STYLE, and the canelé
           explainer is the one whose argument is a table of ratios — so what the frame shows
           is the grid setting type and figures, which is what /swiss is for. `explain` takes
           the 10x essay for the opposite reason (its quiz). -->
      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:calc(var(--unit)*1.5)">
        <figure class="embed c1-12">
          <div class="esrc">
            <a href="/writing/canele/">open the canelé explainer full-screen</a>
          </div>
          <div class="embed-frame">
            <iframe src="/writing/canele/" title="A canelé explainer set by the /swiss skill, live"
              loading="lazy"></iframe>
          </div>
          <!-- The caption is a LABEL, not a second link (annotation: "still have double links, on
               top here and then on bottom, on most of these like embed links — should only have 1
               i think"). The `.esrc` above the frame keeps the link, because an embed is tall and
               the one actionable thing must not sit a screenful below it. Same fix as recipes. -->
          <figcaption>What makes a canelé custardy — set by the skill, live in the frame</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <!-- Essays -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="c1-12"><h2>Written with it</h2></div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
        <div class="links">
          <a href="/writing/10x-is-a-loop/">
            10x is a loop, not a prompt <span class="arrowc">→</span>
            <span class="lu">on AI-assisted engineering, evidence first</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/writing/canele/">
            What makes a canelé custardy <span class="arrowc">→</span>
            <span class="lu">one ratio, from six recipes</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE B close: the facts block as a colophon — what the thing is made of, read after
           the work. On a Type A page the same device is the masthead instead. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="facts">
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Form</div>
            <div class="ftx">
              One <b>SKILL.md</b> holding the design tokens, the grid, and a MUST/MUST&nbsp;NOT
              checklist the agent works through. Output is a single self-contained HTML file.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Source</div>
            <div class="ftx">
              Private repo. The pages here are its output; ask if you want to read the rules.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
